Under federal law, a baby can be covered through Familienversicherung (free family cover for dependants) in the statutory health insurance of a parent who is a member. SGB V §10(1) says the children of members are insured ("Versichert sind ... die Kinder von Mitgliedern") if the child:
1. has their residence or habitual abode in Germany;
2. is not insured in their own right under the categories listed in § 5 Absatz 1, and is not voluntarily insured;
3. is not exempt from, or freed from, compulsory insurance;
4. is not self-employed as a main occupation;
5. has no total income that regularly exceeds one seventh of the monthly reference figure under § 18 of the Fourth Book.

Under §10(2) No. 1, children are insured up to their 18th birthday ("bis zur Vollendung des achtzehnten Lebensjahres"). The later numbers of §10(2) extend this in the cases they list.

The law also sets out an exclusion. Under §10(3), children are not insured if the other parent is a spouse or civil partner of the member, is related to the child, and "nicht Mitglied einer Krankenkasse ist" (is not a member of a statutory health insurer), and that parent's total income both regularly exceeds one twelfth of the annual earnings ceiling ("Jahresarbeitsentgeltgrenze") per month and is regularly higher than the member's total income.

On the practical side, the Familienportal says the birth certificate is needed for, among other things, "Anmeldung bei der Krankenkasse" (registering with the health insurer). The page says the child must be registered within one week at the register office ("Standesamt") responsible for the place of birth, and that this office issues the birth certificate. The page also says that many birth clinics have guides ("Lotsinnen und Lotsen") who explain the benefits available to you and help with filling in applications. The service is free and voluntary.

Whether your baby is covered is not something these excerpts can decide. The health insurer of the parent who is a member decides this. You can ask that insurer for binding information about your case. The excerpts do not describe the registration form or the steps the insurer takes. They also do not cover private health insurance, or how to insure a child who falls under the §10(3) exclusion. Ask the health insurer about these.
